        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it bad to seal a driveway every year?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most residential asphalt driveways, yes. Annual sealing builds up layers faster than traffic wears them off, and the thick crust tends to crack and flake. Every two to three years is enough for typical household use. Commercial lots or very busy shared drives may need more frequent coats, but a family driveway generally does not."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know if my driveway needs sealing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Do the water test. Pour a little water on several spots, including the tire paths. If it beads or puddles, the sealer is still working. If it soaks in quickly and darkens the asphalt, the coat has worn through. Other signs include uniform gray fading, loose stones at the edges and fluid stains that soak in fast."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does sealing actually make asphalt last longer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Sealing slows oxidation from sunlight and keeps water, oil and fuel from attacking the binder, which helps the surface stay flexible. The bigger lifespan gains come from filling cracks promptly so water cannot reach the base. Sealer plus crack filling is the combination that extends a driveway's life; sealer alone over open cracks does much less."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does driveway sealer last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Budget asphalt emulsions often last about one to two years. Premium emulsions with polymers and sand typically last two to four years. Acrylic and polymer sealers often land around three years. Actual life depends on sun exposure, traffic, climate and how well the coat was applied, so use the water test rather than relying on the label alone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I seal a concrete driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on your climate and use. In areas with freeze-thaw cycles and road salt, a penetrating sealer helps prevent scaling and is worth applying every five to ten years per the label. In mild, dry climates, plain concrete can go a long time without sealer. Decorative and stamped concrete usually benefits from a film sealer for color."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I seal just the tire paths?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, many owners spot-seal worn tire paths and turning areas between full coats. Clean the area well, apply a thin coat, and feather the edges so the touch-up blends in. The color may not match perfectly at first, but it usually evens out within a few weeks as the surrounding surface weathers."
          }
