    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sealcoating flyers tend to arrive every spring, and the implied message is that the driveway needs a coat every year. For most homes it does not. A well-applied asphalt sealer on a residential drive usually holds up for two to three years, and coating it more often adds layers that crack and peel on their own. The better approach is to read the surface, not the calendar. This guide gives the typical intervals by surface and sealer type, then the simple checks that tell you whether your driveway is ready now or can wait.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer, Surface by Surface
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Asphalt:</strong> every two to three years for a typical residential driveway, with the first coat six to twelve months after installation. <strong>Concrete:</strong> every two to three years for film-forming acrylic sealers, or roughly every five to ten years for penetrating silane and siloxane sealers, depending on the product's rating. <strong>Pavers:</strong> about every three to five years with a quality joint-stabilizing sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those intervals assume normal household traffic, decent drainage and a sealer applied in the right conditions. A drive in hot sun with heavy use and winter salt will sit at the short end. A shaded, lightly used drive in a mild climate can often stretch past the long end. The ranges are a starting point for a check, not an appointment.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical interval</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">First coat</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main thing it protects against</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-12 months after paving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">UV oxidation, water, fuel and oil</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete, acrylic film sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">After full cure, often 28 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stains, water, surface wear</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete, penetrating sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years per label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">After full cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water absorption, freeze-thaw, salt</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete or brick pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">After efflorescence clears</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Joint sand loss, stains, fading</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice that none of those say once a year. Annual sealing is a contractor business model more than a pavement need, and the section on over-sealing below explains why it can make a driveway look worse over time. The exception is commercial lots and heavily used shared drives, where traffic wears a coat much faster than a family car does.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a single rule that covers most asphalt driveways, use this one: seal when water stops beading on the surface and the black has faded to dark gray across most of the drive. That usually lands between year two and year three. The rest of this guide shows how to run that check properly and what can push the timing either way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="asphalt-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Five Signs an Asphalt Driveway Is Due
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>1. The water test fails.</strong> Pour a cup of water on a few spots, including the main tire paths. On a sealed surface it beads or sits in a puddle for several minutes. If it darkens the asphalt and soaks in within a minute or so, the sealer layer is worn through and water is reaching the binder. This is the most reliable check you can do, and it takes thirty seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>2. The color has gone gray.</strong> Sunlight oxidizes asphalt binder and turns fresh black pavement gray and dull. Some fading on a sealed drive is cosmetic, but when the gray is uniform and the aggregate stones start to look exposed and sharp, the surface is losing binder. <strong>3. Loose stones appear.</strong> Small pebbles collecting at the edge of the drive or in the garage are a sign of raveling, where the binder no longer holds the aggregate.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>4. Hairline cracks are spreading.</strong> Fine cracks wider than about an eighth of an inch let water into the base. They should be filled before sealing, and their spread is a sign the surface needs protection. Our guide to the <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">best driveway crack fillers</a> covers which product fits which crack width. <strong>5. Fluid stains soak in fast.</strong> On a sealed drive, a fresh oil drip sits on top for a while. On a bare one, it darkens the asphalt almost immediately and starts to soften the binder.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If water still beads, wait.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A driveway that passes the water test in the tire paths does not need a new coat yet, even if it has faded a shade. Recheck each spring and fall; most owners find the answer changes somewhere in year two or three.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            One sign that does not mean you need sealer: alligator cracking, potholes, or areas that sink or heave. Those are structural problems in the base, and sealer only hides them for a season. If you see a pattern of interconnected cracks like a reptile's back, get a paving contractor's opinion on patching or resurfacing before spending money on coating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Happens When You Seal Too Often
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each coat of asphalt emulsion leaves a film roughly the thickness of a few sheets of paper. Applied every two to three years, older coats wear away under tires and weather at about the rate new ones go on. Applied every year, they stack. After several seasons, the drive carries a thick, rigid crust of old sealer sitting on top of the asphalt rather than bonded into it.
          </p>

          <p>
            That crust does not flex with the pavement the way the asphalt beneath does. Owners commonly report a fine web of surface cracks that looks like a dried riverbed, or patches that flake off in sheets, especially in the tire paths and where cars turn. Those cracks are in the sealer, not the asphalt, but they look bad and they invite another coat, which makes the problem worse.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thick buildup can also become slippery when wet, particularly on sloped drives, and it hides the actual condition of the pavement underneath. If your drive has a shiny, plasticky look and cracks that do not line up with anything in the asphalt, it may be over-sealed. The fix is to stop sealing for a few years and let traffic wear the excess down, then return to a normal interval.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a cost side as well. A DIY coat on a two-car drive typically runs a few hundred dollars in materials, and a professional job often lands in the low-to-mid hundreds or more depending on size and region. Cutting from annual to every third year saves real money without any loss of protection. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">what it costs to seal a driveway</a> has the current numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-shifts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Pushes Your Interval Shorter or Longer
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Sun exposure</strong> is the biggest single factor for asphalt. A south-facing drive in full sun loses its coat faster than one shaded by trees or the house, because UV breaks down the sealer and the binder underneath. In the Sun Belt and at high elevations, expect the short end of the range or slightly less.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Freeze-thaw cycles</strong> work on any small crack that lets water in. Water expands about nine percent when it freezes, so a hairline crack becomes a wider one over a winter of repeated freezing and thawing. Northern drives benefit more from consistent sealing and crack filling, even if the coat looks fine cosmetically. <strong>De-icing salt</strong> and the slush that drips off cars add chemical wear on top of that.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Traffic</strong> matters in a concentrated way. Two commuter cars on a short drive wear the tire paths and turning area far faster than the edges. Many owners can extend the full-drive interval by touching up only the worn paths every second year. Trucks, trailers and RVs that park in one spot also leave impressions in fresh or thick coats, especially in hot weather.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Drainage and shade</strong> push the other way on the wear front but add their own issues. A shaded drive holds less heat, so the coat lasts longer, but it stays damp, which encourages moss and algae. If yours is green in spots, our guide on <a href="/how-to-remove-moss-from-asphalt-driveway/">removing moss from asphalt</a> covers how to clear it before the next coat.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Effect on Interval</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">-1 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Full sun, hot climate</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">-1 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Heavy traffic or salt</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">+1 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mostly shaded drive</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">+1 yr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Light use, mild climate</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sealer-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Sealer You Use Sets the Clock
          </h2>

          <p>
            Budget asphalt emulsion sealers, the low-cost five-gallon pails at home centers, have low solids content and usually last one to two years. Premium emulsions with more solids, added polymers and sometimes sand typically claim two to four years, and labels on some high-end products go further. The difference in lifespan often makes the more expensive pail cheaper per year of protection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Refined coal tar sealers have historically been the longest-lasting and most resistant to gasoline and oil, but they are now banned or restricted in a number of states and municipalities because of PAH concerns, so many homeowners cannot buy them locally. Acrylic and polymer-modified sealers are generally durable and resist UV well, with many rated in the three-year range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Application quality matters as much as chemistry. Two thin coats outlast one thick coat, because each thin layer cures fully and bonds to the one below. A coat spread in cold or damp conditions can fail inside a year no matter how good the product is. If you want the details on weather windows, see our guide to <a href="/what-temperature-to-seal-driveway/">what temperature to seal a driveway</a> and the companion on <a href="/rain-after-driveway-sealing/">rain after sealing</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you compare products, look at the label's stated coverage per pail, recommended number of coats, and any durability claim, then do the arithmetic for cost per year. Our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">asphalt driveway sealer rankings</a> list those figures side by side, which makes it easy to see whether a pricier pail actually earns its keep on your drive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="new-driveways" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. New Driveways: The First Coat Is a Special Case
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fresh asphalt is still releasing light oils from the binder for months after paving. Sealing too early traps those oils and can lead to poor adhesion and a soft surface. Most paving contractors and sealer manufacturers recommend waiting at least six months and often up to a year before the first coat, so the surface can cure and the oils can evaporate.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple test helps: if the asphalt still has a rich, glossy black look and a faint petroleum smell on a hot day, it is not ready. When the surface has dulled slightly and water no longer beads on its own, it is a good candidate for its first coat. Your paving contractor's recommendation should take priority, since mix designs vary.
          </p>

          <p>
            New concrete has its own waiting period. Most sealer labels call for the concrete to cure for about 28 days, and some penetrating sealers allow earlier application. Sealing too early can trap moisture and cause a cloudy or white finish with film-forming products. Our guide to <a href="/when-should-you-seal-a-new-asphalt-driveway/">when to seal a new asphalt driveway</a> walks through the timeline in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            After the first coat on new asphalt, many owners do a second coat about two years later, then settle into a two-to-three-year rhythm based on the water test. Keep a note of the date and product on your phone. It makes the next decision much easier, and it helps if you ever need to compare how different products held up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concrete-pavers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Concrete and Paver Driveways
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plain broom-finished concrete in a mild, dry climate can go many years without sealer and look fine. Sealing earns its keep where winters bring freeze-thaw cycles and road salt, which can cause surface flaking called scaling, or where oil stains are a recurring problem. In those situations, a penetrating sealer every five to ten years, per the label, is a sensible baseline.
          </p>

          <p>
            Film-forming acrylic sealers add gloss and color depth, which is why they are popular on stamped and decorative concrete. The tradeoff is a shorter life, often two to three years in sun and traffic, and they can become slippery when wet unless an anti-slip additive is used. When the gloss dulls and water no longer beads, it is time to clean and recoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paver driveways need sealer mainly to lock joint sand in place and to resist stains. Most paver sealers last around three to five years. Recoat when the joints start losing sand or weeds appear between stones, and use the same chemistry as the previous coat to avoid compatibility problems. Water-based and solvent-based paver sealers do not always play well together.
          </p>

          <p>
            The water test works on concrete too: sprinkle water and watch whether it beads or darkens the surface. For deeper guidance on products and application, our pages on the <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">best concrete driveway sealers</a> and <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">how to seal a concrete driveway</a> cover each step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ten-year-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. A Realistic 10-Year Plan for an Asphalt Drive
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is what sensible maintenance looks like for a typical asphalt driveway over its first decade. It assumes normal household traffic in a climate with real winters. Adjust the timing with the water test rather than following the years blindly.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Year</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Task</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keep heavy loads off edges in hot weather</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">First seal, two thin coats</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">After the surface dulls and oils have cured</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inspect, fill cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spot-seal tire paths if worn</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3-4</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full reseal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">When water stops beading</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inspect, fill cracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean oil spots promptly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">6-7</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full reseal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Consider one coat if the old one is still intact</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">8-10</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inspect, fill, reseal as needed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Watch for structural cracking</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Crack filling is the step that does the most for the pavement's lifespan, because water in the base is what eventually destroys a driveway. Fill cracks each spring or fall when they appear, even in years when you are not sealing. The sealer is mostly about protecting the surface; the crack filler protects the structure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between coats, keep the surface clean, sweep off debris that holds moisture, and wipe up fuel and oil drips promptly. A simple cardboard or mat under a car that leaks will save a patch of asphalt. If you are weighing whether sealing is worth doing at all, our look at <a href="/sealing-asphalt-driveway-pros-and-cons/">the pros and cons of sealing asphalt</a> lays out both sides.
          </p>

          <p>
            The big picture is that a properly maintained asphalt driveway often lasts 20 to 30 years, while one left bare and unrepaired can need major work much sooner. The difference comes from consistent, moderate care: water out, cracks filled, and sealer applied when the surface asks for it. More is not better; on time is better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing on a fixed annual schedule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coating every year stacks sealer into a thick, brittle crust that cracks and peels on its own. It also costs two to three times as much over a decade as sealing when needed. Use the water test each spring and fall, and seal when water soaks in rather than beading.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over cracks without filling them</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer is too thin to bridge cracks much wider than a hairline. Coating over them leaves a path for water into the base, and freeze-thaw cycles widen them further. Clean and fill cracks a day or two before sealing so the filler has set before the topcoat goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing new asphalt too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fresh asphalt releases oils for months after paving. Sealing during that period traps them and can weaken adhesion. Wait at least six months, and often a full year, until the surface dulls and loses its glossy look, unless your paving contractor gives a different timeline.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating a structurally failing drive</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Alligator cracking, potholes and sinking areas mean the base is failing. A coat of sealer may make the drive look better for a season, but it does nothing for the underlying problem. Get a paving contractor's opinion on patching or resurfacing before spending money on sealer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying one heavy coat instead of two thin ones</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thick single coat dries on top before the layer below has cured, which leads to tracking, soft spots and early failure. Two thin coats, applied with enough drying time between them, bond better and last longer. Follow the coverage rate on the pail rather than pouring extra.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to seal a driveway every year?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most residential asphalt driveways, yes. Annual sealing builds up layers faster than traffic wears them off, and the thick crust tends to crack and flake. Every two to three years is enough for typical household use. Commercial lots or very busy shared drives may need more frequent coats, but a family driveway generally does not.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my driveway needs sealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Do the water test. Pour a little water on several spots, including the tire paths. If it beads or puddles, the sealer is still working. If it soaks in quickly and darkens the asphalt, the coat has worn through. Other signs include uniform gray fading, loose stones at the edges and fluid stains that soak in fast.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing actually make asphalt last longer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sealing slows oxidation from sunlight and keeps water, oil and fuel from attacking the binder, which helps the surface stay flexible. The bigger lifespan gains come from filling cracks promptly so water cannot reach the base. Sealer plus crack filling is the combination that extends a driveway's life; sealer alone over open cracks does much less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does driveway sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Budget asphalt emulsions often last about one to two years. Premium emulsions with polymers and sand typically last two to four years. Acrylic and polymer sealers often land around three years. Actual life depends on sun exposure, traffic, climate and how well the coat was applied, so use the water test rather than relying on the label alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal a concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your climate and use. In areas with freeze-thaw cycles and road salt, a penetrating sealer helps prevent scaling and is worth applying every five to ten years per the label. In mild, dry climates, plain concrete can go a long time without sealer. Decorative and stamped concrete usually benefits from a film sealer for color.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal just the tire paths?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, many owners spot-seal worn tire paths and turning areas between full coats. Clean the area well, apply a thin coat, and feather the edges so the touch-up blends in. The color may not match perfectly at first, but it usually evens out within a few weeks as the surrounding surface weathers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What time of year is best for resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Late spring through early fall in most regions, on a day when air and pavement are at least 50°F and stay there for 24 hours, with no rain forecast. In hot southern climates, spring and fall are often easier than midsummer. Avoid late-season jobs where nights drop into the 40s, since the coat may not cure fully.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

